## Answer

**No**, $f_n(x) \to 0$ almost everywhere does **not** imply $\int_a^b f_n(x)\,dx \to 0$. A counterexample suffices.

### Counterexample

Take $[a,b]=[0,1]$ and define
$$
f_n(x) = n \cdot \mathbf{1}_{[0,\,1/n]}(x), \qquad n = 1,2,3,\dots
$$
Each $f_n$ is a step function, hence integrable on $[0,1]$.

**A.e. convergence to 0.** Fix any $x \in (0,1]$. Choose $N$ so that $1/N < x$. Then for every $n \ge N$, we have $x \notin [0,1/n]$, so $f_n(x) = 0$. Thus $f_n(x) \to 0$ for every $x \in (0,1]$. The only point where convergence may fail is $x=0$ (where $f_n(0)=n\to\infty$), but $\{0\}$ is a null set. Therefore
$$
f_n \to 0 \quad \text{a.e. on } [0,1].
$$

**Integrals do not tend to 0.** For every $n$,
$$
\int_0^1 f_n(x)\,dx = \int_0^{1/n} n\,dx = n \cdot \frac{1}{n} = 1.
$$
Hence $\int_0^1 f_n(x)\,dx = 1$ for all $n$, which converges to $1 \neq 0$.

### Justification

The implication fails because almost-everywhere convergence alone provides **no control over the size of the functions**. The mass of $f_n$ can concentrate on sets of vanishing measure while the function values grow correspondingly, keeping the integral bounded away from $0$.

For the implication $\int_a^b f_n \to 0$ to hold, an additional hypothesis is needed — for instance, the hypotheses of the **Dominated Convergence Theorem** (existence of an integrable $g$ with $|f_n|\le g$ a.e.), or uniform integrability. Under domination, $|f_n|\le g$ with $g$ integrable forces $f_n \to 0$ a.e. to imply $\int f_n \to 0$ by DCT. Without such a bound, the counterexample above shows the conclusion is false.

